window.YANBIAN_NEWS_FALLBACK=[{"title":"Xi says positive interaction between major countries not an option but a must","desc":"Chinese President Xi Jinping on Thursday said that positive interaction between major countries is \"not optional, but a must.\"","full":"Chinese President Xi Jinping on Thursday said that positive interaction between major countries is \"not optional, but a must.\"\nXi made the remarks at a summit with Russian President Vladimir Putin and U.S. President Donald Trump in Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province.\n\"Humanity, living in the same global village, is a community with a shared future where we all stand to benefit or lose together. In this global village, major countries shoulder special responsibilities,\" he said.\nIn recent years, China has been promoting the China-Russia comprehensive strategic partnership of coordination for the new era, and actively developing the constructive China-U.S. relationship of strategic stability, with a view to fostering major-country dynamics featuring peaceful coexistence, overall stability, and balanced development to the benefit of each other and the world at large, he said.\nNoting that transformation not seen in a century is accelerating across the globe, Xi said the world is not peaceful and testing times reveal responsibilities on one's shoulders.\nAs major countries and permanent members of the United Nations Security Council, China, Russia and the United States should set good examples, work together to meet global challenges, jointly uphold world peace and stability, and make greater contributions to the progress of humanity.\nXi hosts welcome banquet for guests attending APEC meeting\nChina is advocate, promoter, forerunner in Asia-Pacific economic cooperation: Xi","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/china/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7428221.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/Z3Qc-hfneuqn7427311.jpg","column":"China","date":"2026-11-19 19:01:26","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Australian mayor's letter highlights 20 years of ties with China's Dali","desc":"A congratulatory letter from an Australian mayor has highlighted two decades of friendship between Mildura, in southeastern Australia, and Dali in China’s southwestern Yunnan provi","full":"(ECNS) -- A congratulatory letter from an Australian mayor has highlighted two decades of friendship between Mildura, in southeastern Australia, and Dali in China’s southwestern Yunnan province, as the prefecture marks its 70th anniversary.\nMildura Mayor Helen Healy congratulated Dali Bai Autonomous Prefecture and noted the two areas’ shared strengths in agriculture and tourism, according to a statement from Dali authorities Wednesday.\nEducation is central to the relationship. More than 400 students and 80 teachers from Mildura have visited Dali through a sister-school exchange program, Healy said in the letter. Students and teachers from Dali have also made reciprocal visits to Australia in the past 15 years.\nHealy said in the letter she hopes the education partnership will continue for future generations.\nBeyond its ties with Mildura, Dali has expanded international exchanges through events such as the Global Ecological Civilization Forum (Erhai) and the Lancang-Mekong Cooperation Dali Marathon. Building on its cultural heritage and international partnerships, the prefecture is seeking to strengthen its role as a gateway for cooperation with South and Southeast Asia.\nDali opens first direct air route to Kuala Lumpur","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427992.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/BttP-hfneuqn7427976.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 16:16:12","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"U.S. business leaders see APEC as opportunity to deepen China-U.S. cooperation","desc":"The Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) forum has long promoted free and open trade and investment, helping foster regional growth and shared prosperity, U.S. business leaders","full":"(ECNS) -- The Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) forum has long promoted free and open trade and investment, helping foster regional growth and shared prosperity, U.S. business leaders told China News Service during a series of APEC meetings in Shenzhen, Guangdong Province, from Nov. 16 to 20.\nThey described APEC as a forum for facilitating regional trade and strengthening policy coordination. They called for closer China-U.S. cooperation in areas such as digital payments and paperless trade to support growth across the Asia-Pacific.\nMichael Hart, president of the American Chamber of Commerce in China, said the meetings brings business executives and government leaders together.\nFor U.S. companies, the meetings offer a chance to better understand China's long-term market prospects and identify business opportunities, he said.\nHart also said enterprises are focusing on opportunities and innovation in China, including the rise of young, competitive Chinese companies in emerging industries.\n\"One of the things happening in Shenzhen is a good example of how fast China has changed and innovated. We've got the headquarters of a number of Chinese companies here and it really shows the innovation going on in China,\" he said.\nHart noted that many U.S. companies operate in Shenzhen and elsewhere in the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area.\n\"It's important for U.S. companies to have clear rules from both sides from their home country and also from China so they understand the environment they can operate in,\" he said.\nSean Stein, president of the U.S.-China Business Council (USCBC), said APEC's significance extended beyond China-U.S. ties to the entire Asia-Pacific region. One of its main goals was to make the cross-border movement of goods smoother and more efficient, he said.\nHe cited high-tech products such as robots, noting that their components could cross borders multiple times, moving through China, Malaysia, Vietnam, Singapore and the United States before returning to China.\n\"The more complex the supply chain, the more important APEC becomes,\" he said.\nStein said progress through APEC would be gradual. \"What we'll see coming out of APEC are many small, incremental changes and recommendations that add up to a great deal in the aggregate.\"\nShenzhen companies stand to benefit substantially from APEC efforts to make trade easier and more efficient. The gains would extend to economies across APEC and consumers around the world, he said.\nThe U.S. business leaders also stressed that closer cooperation with China would strengthen bilateral trade and support wider regional cooperation and growth.\nStein said APEC could better achieve its goals with China and the U.S. working together. Digital payments and paperless trade were among the areas where the two sides could make progress.\n\"Think about how many times goods cross borders every single second in APEC. These little steps are going to make every single one of those transactions a little bit easier, cheaper, faster, and more efficient, but the real impact is going to come from the U.S. and China working together on this and that's what we expect to see out of APEC.\"\nHart identified sustainability, supply chains and economic resilience as priority areas for China-U.S. cooperation. China's market and continued innovation offered companies in both countries opportunities to work together, he said.\nAgriculture is another key part of China-U.S. trade. Carlos Salinas, executive director for East Asia at the U.S. Soybean Export Council (USSEC), said China remained a key market for U.S. soybeans and that American farmers hoped to keep selling there.\nU.S. companies in other sectors also see opportunities in China's growth. Having operated in China for more than four decades, HP continues to look for ways to expand its business.\nBrittany Masalosalo, chief public policy officer at HP Inc., said China is an important part of HP's global manufacturing supply chain and a major hub for research, development and innovation. She said talent from Chinese universities and an active community of local innovators create opportunities for partnerships with multinational companies.\nForeign business leaders eye easier trade within Asia-Pacific, closer collaboration with China at Sh","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427840.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/Z3Qc-hfneuqn7427311.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 15:37:49","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"AI and automation reshape China's wooden toy manufacturing hub","desc":"Artificial intelligence and automated machinery are transforming wooden toy production in Yunhe County in eastern China’s Zhejiang province, as local manufacturers seek to improve ","full":"(ECNS) -- Artificial intelligence and automated machinery are transforming wooden toy production in Yunhe County in eastern China’s Zhejiang province, as local manufacturers seek to improve efficiency and strengthen their position in global markets.\nYunhe, one of China’s largest wooden toy manufacturing centers, produces toys sold in 82 countries and regions, including the United States and Japan. Local government figures put the county’s share of global wooden toy production at about 40%.\nAt Zhejiang Yobell Toys, AI tools built into the company’s management system can analyze product images, identify components and calculate how to cut wooden panels, reducing manual planning and helping cut material waste.\nAutomation is also changing factory operations. At Zhejiang Jincheng Toy Group, a digital management system tracks production, and CNC carving machines, automated guided vehicles and robots handle tasks from processing components to moving materials.\nChinese-made robots impress global mayors in Ningbo with skills of drumming, calligraphy and screwdr\nEmbodied AI events to highlight 2026 China Robot Competition in Hangzhou","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427793.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/wAL8-hfneuqn7427789.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 15:08:22","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"China-Myanmar border port records more than 1.5 million passenger crossings this year","desc":"Meng'a Port on the China-Myanmar border in southwestern China's Yunnan province has recorded more than 1.5 million passenger crossings so far this year, the Mengkang Border Inspect","full":"(ECNS) -- Meng'a Port on the China-Myanmar border in southwestern China's Yunnan province has recorded more than 1.5 million passenger crossings so far this year, the Mengkang Border Inspection Station said Wednesday.\nThe port, in Menglian County in Pu'er, is an important land gateway linking southwestern Yunnan with Myanmar and other Southeast Asian countries. It is a key channel for cross-border travel, trade and cooperation.\nPassenger and vehicle traffic at the port has kept rising this year, border authorities said, with operations running smoothly.\nTo handle the growing flow, the station's Meng'a branch has expanded online arrival card services for foreign visitors and introduced a system combining online pre-processing with on-site queue numbers. Authorities also monitor traffic patterns and forecast peak passenger flows during major holidays and ethnic festivals to help travelers plan their crossings.\nChina sentences 3 from northern Myanmar telecom fraud ring to death\nMyanmar jadeite auction opens in China's Ruili for first time\nMyanmar's leader Min Aung Hlaing to make first official visit to Thailand in August","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427781.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/Z3Qc-hfneuqn7427311.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 15:03:16","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"A cherry's journey across the Pacific highlights stronger supply chains among APEC economies","desc":"In November, as summer begins in the Southern Hemisphere, cherries are ripening on the western slopes of the Chilean Andes. The race is then on to ship the fruit nearly 20,000 kilo","full":"(ECNS) -- In November, as summer begins in the Southern Hemisphere, cherries are ripening on the western slopes of the Chilean Andes. The race is then on to ship the fruit nearly 20,000 kilometers to China. The first shipments of the season on the cherry express service are expected to arrive at Nansha Port in Guangzhou in early December.\nCherries from other APEC economies, including Australia and New Zealand, will also arrive in the Chinese mainland in the coming weeks. With an uninterrupted cold chain and faster customs clearance, the cherries can go from ship to shelf at Guangzhou's Jiangnan Fruit and Vegetable Wholesale Market in as little as two hours.\nThe most noticeable changes over the past decade have been a longer window to get fresh produce to market and lower losses, said Li Yan, customs affairs director at the international logistics center of Joy Wing Mau Fruit Technologies Corporation Limited. For selected products, mutual recognition of inspection and quarantine data has sharply reduced duplicate checks, making deliveries more predictable and reliable.\nGuangzhou Port has run the Chilean cherry express service for seven consecutive years since 2019 and is now one of the Chinese mainland's main gateways for imported cherries.\nAir freight is also strengthening supply chains for fresh produce across the Asia-Pacific. Zheng Jiajun, director for the Chinese mainland at Cathay Group, told China News Service that Cathay Cargo supports trade in high-value perishables among APEC economies. A new air-road express route between Hong Kong and Zhuhai delivers fresh goods directly to major consumer markets in the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area.\nZhu Jiaming, deputy head of the production and business department of Guangzhou Port Company Limited, said that by the end of October, Nansha Port had opened more than 100 shipping routes serving APEC economies. Cargo to and from those economies accounted for about 60% of the port's total throughput. Imports include fruit, frozen goods and timber, while exports include machinery, electronics, home appliances and furniture.\nCold-chain transport also benefits from a well-developed rail network. In Shenzhen, Yantian Port is expanding cold-chain transport services for imports by drawing on rail's extensive reach, high capacity and low costs, making it easier for quality products from across the Asia-Pacific to reach Chinese consumers.\nFrom South American orchards to dinner tables in China, the journey of a single cherry shows how trade links across the Asia-Pacific are growing stronger.\nWith an integrated network of seaports, airports and sea-rail links, along with streamlined customs procedures and end-to-end cold-chain services, more goods from across the region are reaching consumers faster.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427779.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/Z3Qc-hfneuqn7427311.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 15:02:29","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"China's Guangxi expands publishing ties with ASEAN through AI and copyright trade","desc":"Publishers in southern China’s Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region are increasingly using artificial intelligence to develop and adapt content for Southeast Asian audiences. Their par","full":"(ECNS) -- Publishers in southern China’s Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region are increasingly using artificial intelligence to develop and adapt content for Southeast Asian audiences. Their partnerships now extend beyond books to digital entertainment.\nCompanies showcased AI tools for producing content at a publishing industry conference in Nanning, the capital of Guangxi, on Wednesday. They also discussed closer cooperation with members of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN).\nThe platform has attracted more than 100 publishing organizations and lists over 3,000 titles and other copyrighted products, according to Luo Yutao, deputy head of the publishing house. It helps Chinese works reach Southeast Asian markets and introduces ASEAN publications to Chinese readers.\nThe partnerships are expanding beyond books, animation and film to include online literature, video games and live performances. Publishers are also exploring how AI can help create and adapt content in different languages, according to Shen Chuanliang, a senior publicity official in Guangxi.\nThe conference also featured FLYTOTO, an AI-powered short-drama production platform developed by Guangxi Publishing and Media Group. It helps creators adapt stories and other intellectual property for short-form video.\nShao Ping, general manager of Guangxi Yunhan Feidu Culture and Technology, told China News Service that the platform had attracted more than 1,000 registered users from 20 provincial-level regions in China and several ASEAN countries.\nThe company is refining AI translation tools for Southeast Asian languages to reach more viewers. It has agreed to co-produce short dramas with a Malaysian company, and several short films have premiered in Vietnam, Shao said.\nDigital reading, publishing gain momentum in nation\nChinese 'self-media' accounts penalized for misleading publishing","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427634.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/4xGf-hfneuqn7427610.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 11:54:32","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"China is advocate, promoter, forerunner in Asia-Pacific economic cooperation: Xi","desc":"","full":"Chinese President Xi Jinping on Thursday said China is an advocate, promoter and forerunner in Asia-Pacific economic cooperation, and it is ready to work with all parties to build the pillars that support an Asia-Pacific community.\nXi made the remarks at the 33rd Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Economic Leaders' Meeting, held in Shenzhen in south China's Guangdong Province.\nStressing the importance of stronger economic and trade ties underpinning such a community, Xi said efforts should be made to facilitate trade and investment, advance digitalization, and lower barriers to them.\nOn upgrading connectivity, he called for measures to improve infrastructure networks, enhance coordination in policies and regulations, promote people-to-people exchanges, and foster mutual understanding and closer bonds among peoples.\nEmphasizing innovation, Xi said greater efforts need to be made to build digital infrastructure and promote the broad application of artificial intelligence.\nXi stressed the importance of development, calling on regional economies to advance cooperation on energy and food security, anti-corruption governance across borders, and green and low-carbon transition. He also called for efforts to improve the people's well-being and increase investment in human capital.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/news/politics/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427432.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/Z3Qc-hfneuqn7427311.jpg","column":"Politics","date":"2026-11-19 11:44:11","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Fossils in southern China suggest Homo erectus survived later than thought and lived in rainforests","desc":"Fossils unearthed in two caves in southern China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region suggest that Homo erectus survived there much later than previously thought and could adapt to d","full":"(ECNS) — Fossils unearthed in two caves in southern China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region suggest that Homo erectus survived there much later than previously thought and could adapt to dense tropical rainforests, challenging long-held assumptions about the species' habitat and behavior.\nAn international team of scientists identified eight Homo erectus teeth among nine hominin teeth recovered from the Baxian and Naxian cave sites, about 15 kilometers apart in Chongzuo, Guangxi. The fossils date to between 324,000 and 184,000 years ago, making them the youngest known Homo erectus fossils found in China.\nThe findings, published as two companion papers in the journal Nature on Nov. 18, extend the known fossil record of Homo erectus in China by at least 80,000 years and shed new light on the species' evolution and eventual disappearance in East Asia.\nHomo erectus was one of the longest-surviving archaic human species. Its fossil record stretches from Africa to eastern Eurasia and spans the period from about 1.9 million to 108,000 years ago. However, a scarcity of fossils from southern China, particularly from the Middle Pleistocene, has left significant gaps in scientists' understanding of its geographic distribution and ability to adapt to different environments.\nOf the nine teeth recovered from the two cave sites, eight were identified as Homo erectus through traditional and quantitative analyses of tooth morphology. The ninth specimen, designated PA1796, differs in shape from known Homo erectus, Neanderthal and modern human teeth, and its classification remains uncertain.\nResearchers also analyzed ancient proteins in five teeth from the two sites, including PA1796. The molecular evidence supported the identification of the other teeth sampled as Homo erectus and suggested that they came from a population distinct from other known Middle Pleistocene Homo erectus groups in East Asia, including those found at Zhoukoudian in Beijing and Hexian in Anhui Province.\nThe identity of PA1796 remains an open question. Researchers said it may represent a hominin population that remains poorly documented, but further fossil discoveries will be needed to clarify its classification.\nResearchers estimated the ages using five independent dating techniques applied to cave sediments and associated mammalian fossils.\nThe results date the Homo erectus fossils from Baxian Cave to between 324,000 and 184,000 years ago, and those from Naxian Cave to between 319,000 and 241,000 years ago. Taken together, the findings indicate that Homo erectus inhabited the Chongzuo area at some point between 324,000 and 184,000 years ago.\nPreviously known Homo erectus fossil sites in China were concentrated in the northern and central parts of the country, with reliably dated remains generally older than 400,000 years.\nThe discovery extends the known timeline and geographic range of the species in mainland East Asia, making Chongzuo an important area for studying the late survival and eventual disappearance of Homo erectus in China.\nBeyond extending the timeline, the findings also challenge a longstanding assumption that Homo erectus was largely restricted to open grasslands, savannas and sparse woodlands.\nFurther analysis indicates that the Guangxi population lived in closed-canopy rainforests and that some individuals traveled long distances as children.\nThe evidence suggests that Homo erectus could use dense rainforest habitats and travel widely within them, showing greater ecological flexibility than previously recognized.\nThe findings challenge the view that the species struggled to adapt to closed forests, an assumption that has shaped debates about its survival and extinction.\nThe research also raises questions about the possible coexistence of different archaic human populations in East Asia.\nThe broad dating ranges of the Guangxi fossils, together with the unresolved identity of specimen PA1796, leave open the possibility that Homo erectus and Denisovans overlapped in time and geographic range.\nHowever, researchers stressed that further morphological and paleoproteomic analyses of more hominin fossils from the same period in the Chongzuo area will be needed to determine whether the two populations coexisted there.\nThe findings point to southern China, and East Asia more broadly, as important areas for studying the evolution, interaction and potential interbreeding of different archaic human populations.\nThe research team included scientists from more than 10 research institutions in six countries and was led by researchers from the Institute of Vertebrate Paleontology and Paleoanthropology of the Chinese Academy of Sciences and Shandong University.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427430.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/B1P3-hfneuqn7427691.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 11:20:52","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"China's AI computing capacity surges 146% as national network expands","desc":"China’s computing capacity for artificial intelligence rose 146% year over year to 2,592 exaflops by the end of September, according to the Ministry of Industry and Information Tec","full":"(ECNS) -- China’s computing capacity for artificial intelligence rose 146% year over year to 2,592 exaflops by the end of September, according to the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology. The country is expanding a national network to make computing resources easier to access across regions.\nThe increase reflects the rapid expansion of infrastructure for training and running AI models. One exaflop equals one quintillion floating-point operations per second.\nOfficials discussed the expansion on Wednesday at the 2026 Computing Power Internet Conference in Wuhan, the capital of central China’s Hubei Province.\nChina is linking computing facilities across the country so businesses can access processing power beyond their local data centers.\nHan Xia, a senior official with the ministry’s information and communications technology committee, told the conference that China’s national computing services platform had pooled 362 exaflops of computing capacity by the end of September. The platform had also assigned 11.98 million identifiers to help register and manage computing resources nationwide.\nThe government is working to make computing capacity available across regions and computing architectures so resources can be allocated more efficiently, Han said.\nAt the conference, Hubei officially launched a regional node of China’s national computing network to match computing resources with business demand across the country’s eastern and western regions.\nThe province has built two high-speed data routes, one from Wuhan to Yichang and the other from Wuhan to Shiyan via Xiangyang. Both use 400-gigabit transmission technology to move data between computing facilities, according to Hubei provincial authorities.\nHubei has also introduced subsidies to help companies pay for computing services. Provincial officials identified chip design, health care, scientific research and digital content production among the sectors using the subsidies.\nRISC-V China Summit spotlights AI and high-performance computing\nBeijing, Hangzhou, Shenzhen top China's 2026 AI computing power ranking\nChina launches domestically developed 100,000-card AI computing cluster","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427581.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261119/igKX-hfneuqn7427588.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-19 10:55:46","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Xi hosts welcome banquet for guests attending APEC meeting","desc":"","full":"","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/photo/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427315.shtml","img":"","column":"Photo","date":"2026-11-19 10:22:00","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Full text: Toast remarks by Chinese President Xi Jinping at welcome banquet of 33rd APEC Economic Leaders' Meeting","desc":"","full":"Chinese President Xi Jinping and his wife, Peng Liyuan, on Wednesday hosted a banquet in Shenzhen, south China's Guangdong Province, to welcome guests who are here to attend the 33rd Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Economic Leaders' Meeting.\nThe following is the full text of Xi's toast remarks at the banquet:\nTonight, the shores of the South China Sea are ablaze with light, and Shenzhen is at its autumn best. On the eve of the 33rd APEC Economic Leaders' Meeting, I wish to extend, on behalf of the Chinese government and the Chinese people, our warmest welcome to all distinguished guests.\nI have often said that to see over a few thousand years of China, go to Xi'an; to see 500 years of China, go to Beijing; to see 100 years of China, go to Shanghai. Tonight, let me add this: To see 50 years of China, come to Shenzhen.\nShenzhen is a place with a big story to tell. When I first set foot here in 1978, it was little more than a fishing village. In the blink of an eye, more than four decades have passed. Today, high-rises stretch across the horizon and traffic surges through the streets -- a transformation nothing short of miraculous.\nOpenness is in Shenzhen's DNA. From a remote border town to an international metropolis, from merely attracting foreign capital and technology to actively helping shape international rules -- Shenzhen today has trade links spanning the globe and welcomes an endless stream of businesspeople and travelers. It stands as a window on China's high-standard opening up.\nInnovation is the wellspring of Shenzhen's vitality. From export processing to independent research and development, from \"world factory\" to \"city of innovation\" -- Shenzhen today files up to 20,000 international patent applications a year. Advanced manufacturing and modern services flourish side by side, placing the city at the forefront of China's cluster-based innovation.\nCooperation is the source of Shenzhen's strength. As the people of Shenzhen like to say, \"Once here, Shenzhener forever.\" The city opens its minds and arms to go-getters from around the world and brings together talent from over 100 countries and regions, making it a vibrant hub of win-win cooperation between China and the rest of the world.\nThe Shenzhen story is a microcosm of China's reform and opening up. Generation after generation of builders have started from scratch and worked tirelessly to blaze new trails. They have forged ahead in reform and opening up, unleashed and developed the productive forces, and showcased the great dynamism of Chinese modernization.\nThe Shenzhen story is also a shining example of Asia-Pacific cooperation. Economies across the region have risen above geographic constraints and differences in thinking, thrown themselves into the wide arena of regional cooperation, and created the Asia-Pacific miracle of rapid economic growth.\nSince its founding, APEC has always been known for openness, innovation and cooperation. From the first handshake in Seattle to the historic gathering by Beijing's Yanqi Lake, from the solemn commitment to the Bogor Goals to the broad vision of building an Asia-Pacific community -- APEC has remained attuned to the pulse of our times, spearheaded economic integration in the Asia-Pacific, and helped make this region the most dynamic in the global economy and a vital engine of world growth.\n\"On separate sails, we give our best; even a thousand miles apart, the same wind connects our quest.\" This verse captures perfectly why we have crossed mountains and seas to come together -- united by the shared purpose of building an Asia-Pacific community. Since becoming the APEC Chair, China has hosted over 300 meetings and events across 16 cities in the course of the year, with more than 10,000 delegates from member economies taking part and helping to deliver significant outcomes during the APEC China Year. At tomorrow's Leaders' Meeting, we will discuss steps to expand mutual openness, explore pathways to innovation-driven growth, extend opportunities for result-oriented cooperation across various fields, and strive to deliver more tangible results on the ground. I look forward to discussing with you the great endeavor of Asia-Pacific cooperation and advancing our shared cause of Asia-Pacific development.\nNow, I would like to propose a toast. Let's raise our glasses:\nTo the shared prosperity of the Asia-Pacific;\nTo the great success of the APEC Economic Leaders' Meeting; and\nTo the health of all distinguished guests and your families.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/news/politics/2026-11-19/detail-ihfneuqn7427171.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Politics","date":"2026-11-19 09:34:54","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"China's western land-sea corridor posts 11.9% trade growth in first 10 months","desc":"Foreign trade through China’s New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor rose 11.9% year over year to a record 878.82 billion yuan ($123.4 billion) in the first 10 months of 2026, a","full":"(ECNS) -- Foreign trade through China’s New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor rose 11.9% year over year to a record 878.82 billion yuan ($123.4 billion) in the first 10 months of 2026, according to data from Chongqing Customs.\nThe corridor is a rail, road and shipping network linking China’s western interior to international markets through southern ports, including those in Guangxi, a region bordering Vietnam. Chongqing, a major manufacturing and logistics hub in southwestern China, serves as its operational center.\nExports of machinery and electrical products through the corridor reached 250.01 billion yuan from January to October, up 15.5% from a year earlier. High-tech exports grew faster, up 29.8% to 134.51 billion yuan, the customs figures showed.\nImports also increased, with shipments of metal ores and concentrates up 22.1% to 183.63 billion yuan. These shipments supply raw materials to industries in western China. Agricultural imports climbed 15.9% to 80.72 billion yuan, bringing a wider variety of food products to local consumers.\nThe figures point to the corridor’s growing role in connecting western China’s manufacturers with overseas markets while facilitating imports of raw materials and agricultural products.\nPinglu Canal, key project of western land-sea corridor, to open Sept. 16\nNanning International Railway Port: A strategic hub for the New Western Land-Sea Corridor","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426488.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 18:01:51","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Beijing Daxing airport tops 50 million passengers by  2026","desc":"Beijing Daxing International Airport recorded more than 50 million passengers as of Nov. 17 this year, reaching the milestone 19 days earlier than last year, the airport announced ","full":"(ECNS) -- Beijing Daxing International Airport recorded more than 50 million passengers as of Nov. 17 this year, reaching the milestone 19 days earlier than last year, the airport announced on Wednesday.\nBy Tuesday, the airport had handled 318,500 flights and recorded 50.102 million passenger trips this year, averaging 992 flights and 156,100 passengers per day. Flight movements and passenger traffic rose 4.57 percent and 5.8 percent year on year, respectively.\nInternational and regional passenger traffic reached 5.349 million, up 2.71 percent year on year. The airport also handled 197,600 passengers on Aug. 8, its busiest day since opening.\nThe airport is currently served by 72 airlines and has offered flights to 201 destinations worldwide this year, including 52 international and regional destinations across 34 countries.\nNew international routes launched this year serve Helsinki, Frankfurt, Milan, Lisbon, Bali, Tbilisi and Yakutsk. A new route to Antananarivo, Madagascar, is scheduled to open on Dec. 14.\nDuring the winter-spring flight season, the airport has also added domestic destinations, including Baise, Aksu, Bijie, Hotan and Barkol, while operating more flights on several major routes to meet growing travel demand.\nPassenger trips through Beijing Daxing airport top 3 million this year\nBeijing leads way in improving entry policies as visa-free travel burgeons","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426483.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/_pol-hfneuqn7426473.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 18:00:26","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"2026 Forum on Economic Development Along New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor opens in Chongqing","desc":"The 2026 Forum on Economic Development Along New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor (ILSTC) opened in Chongqing on Tuesday. Chinese and foreign guests gathered to discuss cooper","full":"(ECNS) -- The 2026 Forum on Economic Development Along New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor (ILSTC) opened in Chongqing on Tuesday. Chinese and foreign guests gathered to discuss cooperation and development under the theme \"New Land-Sea Connectivity, Opening-Up for Win-Win: Advancing High-Quality ILSTC Development.\"\nDuring the first 10 months of 2026, the import and export value of provincial-level regions along the New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor reached 878.82 billion yuan ($121.4 billion), up 11.9 percent year-on-year, according to the forum.\nHarry Zevon, Chargé d'Affaires ad interim of the Embassy of the Republic of Ghana in China, said that as trade and investment between Ghana and China continue to grow, transportation and trade facilitation systems need to be upgraded simultaneously.\nGhana particularly hopes to draw on Chongqing's experience to modernize its trade and logistics systems, enhance the competitiveness of Ghana's export enterprises, and help businesses better explore the international market, Zevon said.\nAt the forum, the General Administration of Customs of the People's Republic of China (GACC) released the ILSTC Foreign Trade Index.\nThe index focuses on five dimensions: corridor scale, logistics agglomeration, market connectivity, opening-up benefits, and development potential. Using 2019--the year the Master Plan for the corridor was issued and implemented--as the base period with a base point of 100, it establishes an indicator system that fits the corridor's characteristics to comprehensively reflect its construction outcomes.\nData shows that since 2019, the ILSTC Foreign Trade Index has risen steadily for six consecutive years, climbing to 178.2 points in 2025, continuously strengthening its supporting and driving role in foreign trade development.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426447.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 17:41:29","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"(W.E. Talk) Irina Bokova: Competition should not turn into confrontation; communication does not automatically bring understanding","desc":"\"Europe will miss an opportunity if it does not use the potential for mutually advantageous economic cooperation with China,\" former UNESCO Director-General Irina Bokova told China","full":"By Han Yu, Cao Xufan and Naziyeerke Bahetinuer\n(CNS) -- \"Europe will miss an opportunity if it does not use the potential for mutually advantageous economic cooperation with China,\" former UNESCO Director-General Irina Bokova told China News Service's W.E. Talk when discussing China-EU relations.\nBokova, a recipient of the Lifetime Honorary Award at the second Orchid Awards, recently returned to China. She spoke about China-EU economic, trade and technological cooperation, ways to deepen understanding across cultures, and memories of her first visit to Shenzhen some 15 years ago.\nShe also expressed hope that the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Economic Leaders' Meeting, to be held in Shenzhen later this month, would \"serve as a bridge\" to help ease mistrust and find solutions to pressing problems.\nCompetition Should Not Turn into Confrontation\nAsked about China-EU economic and trade frictions, Bokova said that amid geopolitical shifts, China's rise was not always understood in a non-confrontational way. Competition among countries would always exist, she noted, and the key was how to manage it.\n\"The most important thing, in my mind, is not to transform this into a confrontation.\"\nShe identified several areas for closer China-EU cooperation: joint scientific projects, exploring mutual transfers of technology, and technological innovation.\n\"China has a lot to offer Europe,\" she said. In her view, China's vast market and emerging technologies could also benefit Europe.\nBokova emphasized the need for closer cooperation amid major geopolitical shifts.\n\"China and Europe should aspire to become partners and not deepen the divisions that these geopolitical shifts are bringing to the world,\" she said.\nShe noted some positive signs in the relationship, while acknowledging that competition made further cooperation difficult. Negotiations would not always be easy, she said, but both sides should seek partnership, work through their differences, and avoid further divisions.\nIn October, the second meeting of the China-EU Trade and Investment Consultations and the 17th EU-China Joint Steering Committee Meeting on Science and Technology Cooperation were held in succession, covering trade and economic frictions, scientific and technological innovation, and cooperation mechanisms.\nCommunication Does Not Automatically Bring Understanding\n\"We communicate with each other almost every single minute, but it doesn't mean that we have become more tolerant or more knowledgeable,\" Bokova said. More frequent communication, she noted, did not necessarily lead to greater mutual understanding.\n\"Communication does not automatically bring the results that we want.\"\nShe stressed the importance of education for cultural competence. Exchanges among museums, joint research by historians and scientists, exchanges of university students and professors, and visits to cultural sites in different countries could all help deepen understanding.\nBokova voiced strong support for China's visa-free policy for visitors from many European countries. She also mentioned China's initiatives to encourage visits by young people from abroad, support joint scientific projects, and attract international talent.\nInvolved with many universities herself, she said she had seen how important such exchanges were. People at different levels of society also needed to get to know one another to overcome possible prejudices and mistrust.\n\"It is not just governments that have to play a role,\" she said.\nOn China's international communication efforts, Bokova acknowledged renewed efforts in recent years while noting that they had not always achieved the desired results.\n\"Presenting Chinese culture and China's success to the world is a challenge, but it is a huge opportunity,\" she said.\nShe recalled visiting Chinese exhibitions marking the 60th anniversary of diplomatic relations between China and France, noting the considerable impact they had made.\n\"I think that people have to come and see with their own eyes,\" she stressed.\nBy visiting China themselves, foreign visitors could see the country's development firsthand and gain a deeper understanding of Chinese traditions. Personal experience, in her view, could help people form their own understanding of China rather than rely solely on what some media present.\nOn her first visit to Shenzhen about 15 years ago, a bustling public reading space left a lasting impression on Bokova. People sat reading on the floor and in adjoining areas, while other spaces were set aside for children. She said she still had photographs from that visit.\n\"Shenzhen is a green city. Shenzhen is a learning city,\" she said.\nIn her view, the city's development since China's reform and opening-up has brought together universities, technology, culture and finance, reflecting an emphasis on learning, science and technology, and inclusive development.\nThe 33rd APEC Economic Leaders' Meeting will be held in Shenzhen on Nov. 18 and 19.\nBokova noted that the Asia-Pacific region was growing economically, with many countries embracing technological development. The United States and Russia were also important players in the region. Amid complex regional challenges, countries needed more opportunities for dialogue and cooperation.\nShe said the world faced multiple challenges, with progress toward the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals falling short, alongside climate change and geopolitical shifts. Against this backdrop, she emphasized China's role as an important supporter of the United Nations and multilateralism.\n\"There is definitely a need for more dialogue,\" Bokova said.\n\"I hope that this meeting here will serve as a bridge,\" she added, expressing hope that the APEC meeting in Shenzhen could help ease mistrust and find solutions to pressing problems.\n\"I think that today, the attention of many people around the world is very much focused on Shenzhen,\" Bokova said.\nIrina Bokova is former Director-General of the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization (UNESCO) and Senior Advisor to the Board of Directors of the Europe-Asia Center.\nShe served as UNESCO Director-General from 2009 to 2017. During her tenure, she helped facilitate the inclusion of 11 Chinese cities in the UNESCO Creative Cities Network and nine major Chinese cultural heritage sites on the UNESCO World Heritage List. She also played a key role in establishing the UNESCO Prize for Girls' and Women's Education in cooperation with China.\nIn 2016, Bokova was named to Forbes' list of the World's 100 Most Powerful Women. In 2020, she was elected an International Honorary Member of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences. In 2025, she received the Lifetime Honorary Award at the second Orchid Awards and the 2025 Chinese Government Friendship Award.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426445.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 17:39:05","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Shenzhen makes payments easier for foreign visitors, from subway rides to tax refunds","desc":"Foreign visitors to Shenzhen can now use international bank cards to pay for subway rides, make payments at tens of thousands of businesses and claim tax refunds at self-service ma","full":"(ECNS) -- Foreign visitors to Shenzhen can now use international bank cards to pay for subway rides, make payments at tens of thousands of businesses and claim tax refunds at self-service machines, as the southern Chinese city upgraded its payment services ahead of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) meetings.\nMore than 41,000 businesses across Shenzhen have accepted foreign-issued bank cards, while over 2,500 stores have offered tax-refund services to eligible overseas visitors, according to Zheng Jingfei, general manager of China UnionPay’s Shenzhen branch.\nBanks and payment providers are offering services including foreign currency exchange, mobile payment assistance and tax refund advice at major hotels, shopping areas and visitor service centers.\nVisitors can now tap contactless Visa, Mastercard, American Express and JCB cards[R1.1] at subway gates across Shenzhen’s metro network without buying a local transit card or exchanging currency first, according to the Shenzhen municipal government.\nThe city has also expanded currency exchange and payment assistance services, giving visitors alternatives to China’s widely used mobile payment apps.\nClaiming tax refunds is also becoming easier. New self-service machines allow eligible overseas visitors to complete the process in about two minutes, compared with about 10 minutes at staffed service counters, according to a Bank of China employee interviewed by China News Service.\nThe machines combine identity verification, document checks and refund processing, reducing the need for visitors to use staffed counters. China’s tax authorities have also reported[R2.1] the introduction of multilingual self-service refund facilities in Shenzhen.\nFor Brazilian tourist Emanuela, the new service made shopping more convenient. After buying electronics at a Shenzhen mall, she claimed her tax refund at a self-service machine.\nEmanuela told China News Service that the machine was easy to use and that both shopping and claiming her tax refund had gone smoothly.\nForeign cards accepted for customs tax payments\nShenzhen has also introduced a new payment option for travelers who need to pay import taxes on personal items exceeding duty-free allowances.\nAt Huanggang Port, a border crossing between Shenzhen and Hong Kong, customs authorities processed China’s first direct customs tax payment using a foreign-issued bank card on Nov. 5,[R3.1] according to Shenzhen Customs.\nThe system allows travelers to pay directly by card at a customs terminal, removing the need to exchange currency or switch to another payment method.\nShenzhen APEC: Experiencing the future tech in robot block\nAPEC CEO Summit 2026 kicks off in Shenzhen with focus on regional cooperation, future prosperity","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426431.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 17:30:28","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Cross-border QR payments boost business for Shenzhen shops","desc":"Foreign visitors to Shenzhen can now pay at small shops by scanning a QR code with their home countries' digital wallets, after overseas services such as PayPal and Vietnam's MoMo ","full":"(ECNS) -- Foreign visitors to Shenzhen can now pay at small shops by scanning a QR code with their home countries' digital wallets, after overseas services such as PayPal and Vietnam's MoMo were linked to WeChat Pay this year, CCTV Finance reported Wednesday.\nAt a bagel shop in Shenzhen's Shekou district, a Vietnamese tourist completed a purchase within seconds using MoMo, a Vietnamese digital wallet. The shop's American owner, Matt, who has lived in Shenzhen for about 16 years, said mobile payments have made transactions faster and more convenient for foreign visitors.\nThe report said the services help small businesses attract international customers and make trade among Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) economies smoother.\nOverseas visitors made 183 million non-cash transactions in Shenzhen worth 26.847 billion yuan in the first 10 months of the year, up 20% in number and 26% in value from a year earlier, according to the Shenzhen branch of the People's Bank of China.\nWeChat Pay now supports more than 60 overseas digital wallets across 16 countries and regions. Dai Haiyong, a cross-border product executive at Tencent Financial Technology, said transactions involving foreign visitors at Shenzhen's small and medium-sized merchants have risen more than 60% this year compared with the same period last year.\nMeanwhile, UnionPay has expanded cross-border QR payment cooperation, allowing two-way QR code payments between China and Indonesia. Similar projects are advancing in several APEC economies, including Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam, Japan and South Korea. UnionPay QR codes are accepted by more than 53 million merchants outside China, the report said.\nChina's Cross-Border Interbank Payment System processed transactions worth 162.3 trillion yuan in the first nine months of the year, up about 20% from a year earlier, according to the central bank.\nThe growing cross-border payment network is reducing transaction costs and improving payment efficiency, providing stronger support for trade and investment across the Asia-Pacific region, the report said.\nShenzhen makes payments easier for foreign visitors, from subway rides to tax refunds","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426429.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 17:29:35","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"China tests two large unmanned cargo aircraft","desc":"The W5000, described as the world's largest unmanned cargo aircraft by payload capacity, completed its maiden flight on Saturday. With a maximum payload of five metric tons, the ai","full":"(ECNS) -- Two Chinese-developed large unmanned cargo aircraft, the W5000 and Changying-8, have completed test flights, Chinese media reported.\nThe W5000, described as the world's largest unmanned cargo aircraft by payload capacity, completed its maiden flight on Saturday. With a maximum payload of five metric tons, the aircraft has a cruising speed of 526 kilometers per hour (327 mph) and a maximum range of 2,600 kilometers.\nPowered by two domestically developed AEP-100 turboprop engines, the W5000 features a cargo hold of more than 65 cubic meters. During its maiden flight, it climbed to an altitude of 1,000 meters and completed a series of tests before landing safely after about 30 minutes.\nMeanwhile, China North Industries Group Corp., which developed the Changying-8, announced Tuesday that the aircraft had completed a cargo delivery and airdrop test.\nThe aircraft can carry up to 3.5 metric tons of cargo and has a maximum range of more than 3,000 kilometers. It can operate in high-altitude environments and take off and land on runways as short as 200 meters.\nDuring the test, the Changying-8 automatically released a cargo pallet from an altitude of 300 meters before returning safely.\nAviation observer Shi Zhongjian told China News Service that the W5000 focuses on high-capacity, cost-efficient commercial logistics, while the Changying-8 specializes in short takeoffs and landings, precision airdrops and emergency deliveries.\nAccording to official estimates, the W5000 could reduce logistics costs per ton-kilometer by more than 60% compared with conventional crewed cargo aircraft of a similar class.\nShi added that the Changying-8's extended range would allow it to fly nonstop from Beijing to Xinjiang, making it suitable for cargo delivery in remote and challenging environments. Shi also said the two aircraft could work together if used by the military, with the W5000 carrying large volumes of supplies to airfields near the front and the Changying-8 delivering them to remote frontline units by short landings or airdrops.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426422.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/X4ko-hfneuqn7426414.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 17:24:32","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"China approves 8 regions for 6G innovation and development pilot program","desc":"China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has approved eight regions to participate in a ministry-provincial collaborative pilot program for 6G innovation and develop","full":"China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has approved eight regions to participate in a ministry-provincial collaborative pilot program for 6G innovation and development.\nThe eight provincial-level regions are Beijing, Hebei, Shanghai, Jiangsu, Zhejiang, Anhui, Hubei and Guangdong, the ministry said.\nThe pilot program aims to pool regional resources and strengthen coordination between central and local authorities to advance 6G technological innovation, industrial development and application.","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426374.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 17:06:17","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Greater Bay Area leads global innovation ranking as regional cooperation grows","desc":"The Shenzhen-Hong Kong-Guangzhou innovation cluster has topped a global innovation ranking for a second straight year, while cities in southern China step up cooperation to link sc","full":"(ECNS) -- The Shenzhen-Hong Kong-Guangzhou innovation cluster has topped a global innovation ranking for a second straight year, while cities in southern China step up cooperation to link scientific research with manufacturing.\nThe cluster ranked first in the World Intellectual Property Organization’s 2026 Global Innovation Index, ahead of Tokyo-Yokohama and San Jose-San Francisco. The Macao-Zhuhai cluster also made the world’s top 100 for a third straight year, ranking 99th.\nThe rankings come as business leaders gather in Shenzhen for the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) CEO Summit on Tuesday and Wednesday, with cross-border innovation and business opportunities among the topics of discussion.\nThe Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area, which includes Hong Kong, Macao and nine cities in Guangdong province, is seeking to combine Hong Kong’s research capabilities with the mainland’s manufacturing capacity and supply chains.\nNearly 3,000 researchers from Guangdong and Hong Kong have participated in a joint university research funding program since 2024, with more than 65% of them early-career researchers, according to Guangdong’s science and technology authorities.\nIn Zhongshan, a manufacturing city near Shenzhen, an industrial park has attracted 30 companies under a partnership model designed to turn research developed in Shenzhen into commercially viable products.\nLu Yang, associate dean of engineering at the University of Hong Kong, told China News Service that Hong Kong could focus on advanced research and pilot production, while neighboring mainland cities handle large-scale manufacturing.\nBeyond research and manufacturing, the Greater Bay Area is seeking to help technology companies expand internationally by combining Guangdong’s industrial capabilities with Hong Kong’s financial services and Macao's commercial connections.\nThe APEC CEO Summit in Shenzhen offers companies an opportunity to explore partnerships across Asia-Pacific economies. Wang Pan, chief financial officer of Shenzhen-based e-commerce technology company Shoplazza, told China News Service that the company hopes to better understand businesses’ digital needs in different markets and identify potential partners.\nBut entering foreign markets requires more than competitive products. Companies must also navigate financing, legal and regulatory requirements. Deng Xiwei, associate dean of the University of Hong Kong’s business school, said Hong Kong’s financial institutions and professional services could help businesses manage these challenges.\nMacao provides another gateway, particularly to Portuguese-speaking markets. Ji Xianzheng, secretary-general of the permanent secretariat of the China-Portuguese-speaking Countries cooperation forum, said Macao’s language expertise, legal services and business networks could help connect mainland Chinese manufacturers with overseas customers.\nGuangzhou South Railway Station sets record with more than 1 million daily passenger trips\n'Shenzhen-Hong Kong-Guangzhou' innovation cluster retains top global ranking","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426293.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/CJQV-hfneuqn7426498.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 16:21:20","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Hong Kong chief executive highlights city's role as gateway to Greater Bay Area","desc":"Hong Kong can serve as a gateway for Asia-Pacific businesses seeking investment and trade opportunities in southern China’s Greater Bay Area, Chief Executive John Lee said at the A","full":"(ECNS) -- Hong Kong can serve as a gateway for Asia-Pacific businesses seeking investment and trade opportunities in southern China’s Greater Bay Area, Chief Executive John Lee said at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) CEO Summit in Shenzhen, Guangdong Province, on Tuesday.\nThe city’s financial services, international links and professional expertise are central to its role in helping companies navigate cross-border business, Lee said.\nThe Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area, which includes Hong Kong, Macao and nine cities in Guangdong province, has a population of more than 88 million and an economic output exceeding $2.1 trillion, according to figures Lee cited in his address.\nTrade between the nine mainland cities and APEC economies reached about $837 billion in the first nine months of the year, up more than 26% from a year earlier, according to the address. High-end manufacturing, electronics, integrated circuits and green products were among the main drivers of regional trade.\nTogether, the region’s manufacturing capacity, capital resources and growing consumer market offer opportunities for Asia-Pacific companies looking to expand operations, develop partnerships and strengthen supply chains, Lee said.\nLee outlined three ways the Greater Bay Area is improving connectivity: expanding transportation infrastructure, aligning business rules and standards and encouraging exchanges of people and talent.\nHe cited the Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macao Bridge and the Guangzhou-Shenzhen-Hong Kong high-speed rail link as examples of infrastructure connecting cities across the region. Efforts to align regulations and standards are also aimed at reducing barriers to cross-border business, he said.\nThe region operates under China’s “one country, two systems” framework, with three legal jurisdictions and three currencies. Lee said the arrangement allows Hong Kong to maintain its distinct economic and legal systems while participating in the wider regional economy.\nLee said Hong Kong’s appeal to international businesses rests on its common law system, free flow of capital and information, competitive tax regime and deep pool of talent. These advantages underpin the city's role as a link between the Greater Bay Area and the wider Asia-Pacific.\nFinance is one of the clearest examples. The address cited Hong Kong’s third-place global ranking in the Global Financial Centres Index and its role in processing more than 75% of global offshore renminbi payments. Lee said these financial services give international companies access to funding and support for structuring cross-border investments and managing currency and operational risks.\nTechnology offers another area for cooperation. The Shenzhen-Hong Kong-Guangzhou innovation cluster topped the World Intellectual Property Organization’s Global Innovation Index for a second straight year, according to Lee’s address. He said Hong Kong can provide businesses with a base to test new technologies, protect intellectual property and expand solutions across the Greater Bay Area.\nLee closed his address by inviting Asia-Pacific businesses to combine their innovation capabilities and market knowledge with the Greater Bay Area’s manufacturing capacity, capital and growing consumer market. Such partnerships, he said, could strengthen regional supply chains and create opportunities for businesses across the region.\nAPEC finance ministers meet in Hong Kong to discuss regional economic stability, transformation\nAPEC CEO Summit 2026 kicks off in Shenzhen with focus on regional cooperation, future prosperity\nGuangdong signs $4.1 billion in Asia-Pacific business deals","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426288.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 16:14:43","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"Trump says he looks forward to visiting China","desc":"U.S. President Donald Trump said Tuesday in Manila that he is looking forward to his upcoming visit to China, according to CNN.","full":"(ECNS) -- U.S. President Donald Trump said Tuesday in Manila that he is looking forward to his upcoming visit to China, according to CNN.\n\"I look forward to being in China. We have some very important things to discuss,\" Trump said.\nHe arrived in Manila on Nov. 17 as part of a trip to Asia.\nHe is expected to travel to Shenzhen in southern China's Guangdong province on Wednesday to attend the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Economic Leaders' Meeting.\nTrump's delegation includes Secretary of State Marco Rubio, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent, U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer and several White House staff members, according to an earlier report by The Hill.\nIn Numbers; APEC Shenzhen meeting to inject fresh momentum into Asia-Pacific cooperation\nAPEC CEO Summit 2026 kicks off in Shenzhen with focus on regional cooperation, future prosperity\nForeign business leaders eye easier trade within Asia-Pacific, closer collaboration with China at Sh","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426282.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/DpEJ-hfneuqn7425772.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 15:59:45","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"},{"title":"EdUHK honors Asian Games medalists, rugby champion hopes to inspire youth","desc":"The Education University of Hong Kong (EdUHK) honored its medal-winning athletes from the 2026 Aichi-Nagoya Asian Games at a ceremony Tuesday, presenting scholarships and certifica","full":"(ECNS) -- The Education University of Hong Kong (EdUHK) honored its medal-winning athletes from the 2026 Aichi-Nagoya Asian Games at a ceremony Tuesday, presenting scholarships and certificates.\nRugby sevens gold medalist Cado Lee Ka-to said he hopes to combine his experience as a professional athlete with what he is learning in his studies to inspire more young people to take up sports.\nLee, who is studying for a master's degree in sports coaching and management, said psychology courses have helped him understand how to ease young athletes' competition pressure.\nFellow gold medalist Chiu Hin-chun, who won the men's lightweight single sculls event, said he hopes to contribute to Hong Kong's sports development.\nEdUHK President John Lee Chi-kin praised the athletes' achievements and encouraged them to show Hong Kong's sporting talent to the world.\nMore than 240 athletes have enrolled in EdUHK programs since 2010, including several Asian Games champions.\nEight Hong Kong Universities ranked in 2026 THE global top list","link":"https://www.ecns.cn/cns-wire/2026-11-18/detail-ihfneuqn7426273.shtml","img":"https://image.cns.com.cn/ecns_editor/transform/20261118/KW3T-hfneuqn7426269.jpg","column":"Ecns wire","date":"2026-11-18 15:41:14","source":"ECNS 中新网英文"}];
